            {
              "type": "reasoning.summary",
              "summary": "**Analyzing confidence metrics**\n\nI’m reflecting on a confidence score of .685. It suggests something stronger, but still relatively low. It seems these scores may depend on survey results and peer evaluations. Most of the reports appear low, potentially due to global observations. I wonder if I can derive a true total from the confidence ratings. It’s interesting that the score is not a guarantee but more about predicted accuracy. I should definitely consider using peer reports as weighted averages for better accuracy.**Interpreting confidence mechanisms**\n\nI'm considering how to handle confidence in the absence of a defined mechanism, possibly using a survey generative model alongside peer recommendations as evidence. The reports seem to suggest that their confidence levels are not guarantees but reflect the information they have from surveys. Each category has its own unique responses. It might be beneficial to combine peer forecasts as standard averages rather than multiplying them, since they all estimate the same state. Peer confidence could serve as a useful summary for inference.**Evaluating forecast models**\n\nI’m trying to figure out if averaging six independent forecasts creates a proper pooled forecast. Under exchangeability, a linear pool could work, but with known independent signals, Bayesian pooling seems more appropriate. However, since confidence values don’t align with posteriors, maybe simply averaging is best. I calculated a peer total of about 1.857, leading to an average of around .37619. The prompt highlights that every observation should only be counted once, which feels crucial for accuracy.",
